# PelizzAI harness (Cursor)

This project uses the **PelizzAI** skills harness. For tasks that need to inspect or change the
project, read `.agents/skills/pelizzai-core/SKILL.md` and then
`.agents/skills/pelizzai-router/SKILL.md`. Purely conceptual questions, with no project context,
may be answered directly.

## Entry contract

The router classifies the `effect`, looks the request up in its table of rows (one row → one head
skill and what follows it), derives the lane for feature/refactor/infra, and adds overlays only by
observable signals. The route (track, lane, head skill, overlays, discovery) is presented as a
**recommendation to ratify** at the kickoff gate; the head skill is invoked only after the "yes". The
harness chooses how to reason; the user decides requirements, scope, UX, architecture, data, risk,
and acceptance.

- `read-only`: inspect without creating branch, state, profile, catalog, report, or bootstrap.
- `write-local`: go through the first write gate before any persistent file.
- `external`: also validate authority, target, reversibility, and confirmation at the action's gate.
- UI/UX/CSS/component/layout/accessibility: apply `pelizzai-interface` as a mandatory overlay,
  including design system, anti-AI-slop, and visual QA.
- Security surfaces, domain skills, and documentation travel with the work through Verification.

For feature/refactor/infra, pick the lane by risk and uncertainty:

- `bounded`: compact plan, no discovery forced;
- `standard`: a plan, with a compact discovery only if a real trade-off remains;
- `exploratory`: full discovery and stress-testing before the plan.

A greenfield product/project is always `exploratory`, even with the stack defined: ask one question
at a time, always recommend the best option, produce and stress-test spec and plan, get approval,
and decide domain skills before implementing. Context7 grounds technical options; it never decides
for the user.

OODA is an adaptive macro technique, not a universal ritual. Debugging triages and contains damage
when needed, using only the hypotheses that discriminate between the plausible causes. TDD, team,
subagents, and interviews are situational as well. The testing strategy depends on the artifact.

## Non-negotiable rules

- Never commit to `main`, `master`, `develop`, `dev`, or a detached HEAD.
- For mutating tasks, create/validate the task branch before state, spec, plan, ADR, code,
  configuration, test, scaffold, or prototype.
- Specs and plans live on the task branch. A later worktree starts from the same existing branch
  after the planning checkpoint.
- Branch base/name, isolation, execution mode (with `team` always visible), and commit strategy
  are recommended and ratified one question at a time; never a silent default.
  `squash-final` only on explicit request, and `destination` is confirmed per task.
- Content is only ready when review, overlays, and Verification validate the same commit and
  record its SHA in `validated-head`. Finish may add only the metadata-only commit of `state.md`.
- Push, PR, deploy, message, discard, and other external actions require the corresponding gate.
- Code in English; reply to the user in the language of the conversation.
- For external libraries, frameworks, and APIs, ground yourself in available official documentation.
- On Windows, translate POSIX examples to PowerShell when needed.
- Follow the full guidelines in `AGENTS.md`.

## File ownership

`.claude/skills/` and `CLAUDE.md` are the canonical sources. `.agents/skills/`, `AGENTS.md`, and
`GEMINI.md` are generated by the portable core `scripts/sync-harness.mjs` (`.ps1`/`.sh` wrappers)
and must not be edited by hand.

This file, `.cursor/rules/pelizzai.mdc`, is a **manual adapter**, not a generated target. Keep it
short and aligned with the `pelizzai-core` and `pelizzai-router` entrypoints.
